1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:07,450
This is a working walkthrough of VForce360, the governed data
lakehouse Edgent builds and operates.

2
00:00:07,970 --> 00:00:16,133
Almost everything in it runs on open demonstration tenants that
need no sign in, so you can reproduce it yourself by opening the

3
00:00:16,133 --> 00:00:18,620
same address and typing the same thing.

4
00:00:19,140 --> 00:00:25,990
All of the data is synthetic. No real student or employee records
appear anywhere in this recording.

5
00:00:26,510 --> 00:00:31,110
Start with the catalog, because everything else in the platform
hangs off it.

6
00:00:31,630 --> 00:00:40,369
One tenant here holds four catalogs. The gold layer carries 29
curated tables, the silver layer carries the 21 conformed tables

7
00:00:40,369 --> 00:00:46,355
behind them, and a separate catalog holds pages that came out of
the document pipeline.

8
00:00:46,875 --> 00:00:52,025
Raw, silver, gold and audit are first class objects with their own
permissions.

9
00:00:52,545 --> 00:00:59,145
Open a single table and you get its whole record. This one reports
chronic absenteeism by school.

10
00:00:59,665 --> 00:01:07,315
Seven columns, each with its type and its source, and a quality
status per column that comes from the checks declared against it.

11
00:01:07,835 --> 00:01:16,297
Note the last column, called suppressed. This table is built to
withhold a rate when the group behind it is too small to report, as

12
00:01:16,297 --> 00:01:18,235
student privacy rules require.

13
00:01:18,755 --> 00:01:25,955
The sample rows are real rows from the table, and the footer states
plainly that they are masked according to policy.

14
00:01:26,475 --> 00:01:32,850
The lineage view answers the question an auditor actually asks,
which is where this number came from.

15
00:01:33,370 --> 00:01:41,448
Every edge resolves to the job that produced it. The platform
derived it from the transformations it ran, so it cannot fall out

16
00:01:41,448 --> 00:01:44,120
of date the way a documented diagram does.

17
00:01:44,640 --> 00:01:51,865
Quality is declared on the table itself, so it travels with the
data rather than living in a separate testing tool.

18
00:01:52,385 --> 00:02:01,410
This table carries four scored checks. School identifier must not
be null. The chronic threshold has to fall between 5 and 20. The

19
00:02:01,410 --> 00:02:08,560
chronic count has to clear a minimum cell size, which is the
suppression rule enforced as data quality.

20
00:02:09,080 --> 00:02:17,173
Three of the four are configured checks that declare a severity,
and all three are set to fail rather than warn, so breaking one

21
00:02:17,173 --> 00:02:19,955
marks the table as not meeting its contract.

22
00:02:20,475 --> 00:02:26,675
The run history is on the same screen, with the result of every
evaluation and when it happened.

23
00:02:27,195 --> 00:02:34,745
Across the whole catalog there are 20 rules over 7 monitored
tables, passing at 99.3 percent.

24
00:02:35,265 --> 00:02:44,198
The interesting line is the one that is not passing. A rule on the
student grade column is at 85.9 percent and is flagged as a

25
00:02:44,198 --> 00:02:44,765
warning.

26
00:02:45,285 --> 00:02:52,235
Now the analyst. This is a question in plain language, asking that
same table for its school level counts.

27
00:02:52,755 --> 00:02:59,155
The answer comes back as rows rather than as a paragraph describing
rows, and look at what came back.

28
00:02:59,675 --> 00:03:08,150
Nine of the ten rows are blank, and each one is marked suppressed.
Only the district total, covering 65 students, was released.

29
00:03:08,670 --> 00:03:17,011
The individual schools were withheld because the group behind each
one is too small to report without identifying somebody. That is

30
00:03:17,011 --> 00:03:22,295
the minimum cell size rule from the previous screen, doing its job
on a live query.

31
00:03:22,815 --> 00:03:31,102
The SQL is shown, and it is always shown. You are never asked to
trust a number you cannot trace back to the statement that produced

32
00:03:31,102 --> 00:03:31,290
it.

33
00:03:31,810 --> 00:03:39,196
The obvious question is whether the masking is just something the
analyst chose to do, and whether writing the query yourself would

34
00:03:39,196 --> 00:03:39,985
get around it.

35
00:03:40,505 --> 00:03:49,669
Open in SQL Editor hands over the exact statement. From that point
it is an ordinary query, and this one has been rewritten by hand to

36
00:03:49,669 --> 00:03:52,405
ask for the suppressed columns directly.

37
00:03:52,925 --> 00:04:01,355
Same ten rows. Same nine blanks. The suppression is enforced on the
read path, so it applies to the analyst, to the editor, and to

38
00:04:01,355 --> 00:04:03,625
anything else that reads the table.

39
00:04:04,145 --> 00:04:10,520
That is the difference between a policy a platform documents and a
policy a platform enforces.

40
00:04:11,040 --> 00:04:17,215
The part of a data platform that matters most to a public
institution is the part that says no.

41
00:04:17,735 --> 00:04:24,685
This is the permissions tab of a governed table, viewed by the
anonymous session we have been using all along.

42
00:04:25,205 --> 00:04:33,350
It does not show an empty list, and it does not show an error. It
states the rule that was not satisfied. You need admin on this

43
00:04:33,350 --> 00:04:35,005
object to view its grants.

44
00:04:35,525 --> 00:04:44,264
The connector configuration answers the same way. A refusal that
names the missing relation is something an administrator can act

45
00:04:44,264 --> 00:04:46,025
on. A blank screen is not.

46
00:04:46,545 --> 00:04:55,153
Here is the identical table and the identical tab, on the address
that requires a sign in, as a service account belonging to this

47
00:04:55,153 --> 00:04:55,620
tenant.

48
00:04:56,140 --> 00:05:04,053
Now the grants are listed. Who holds them, whether they are a user
or a group, what level each one has, and whether the platform has

49
00:05:04,053 --> 00:05:06,990
re-checked them against the live decision engine.

50
00:05:07,510 --> 00:05:12,560
Nothing about the table changed. The only thing that changed is who
is asking.

51
00:05:13,080 --> 00:05:21,005
You do not have to take that on faith. The platform answers a
permission question directly, for any subject and any object.

52
00:05:21,525 --> 00:05:27,875
Asked on behalf of everyone, the shared table is allowed and the
two restricted tables are denied.

53
00:05:28,395 --> 00:05:36,633
Asked on behalf of the service account, all three are allowed, and
each answer carries the reason. Granted viewer directly on this

54
00:05:36,633 --> 00:05:39,295
table. Granted owner directly on that one.

55
00:05:39,815 --> 00:05:48,237
Same engine, same objects, two subjects, opposite verdicts, with
the grant that produced each verdict written out. That is what

56
00:05:48,237 --> 00:05:52,415
makes an access decision auditable rather than merely enforced.

57
00:05:52,935 --> 00:06:01,239
Every one of those decisions is recorded. This is the compliance
ledger, and the subject column contains both the anonymous session

58
00:06:01,239 --> 00:06:02,760
and the service account.

59
00:06:03,280 --> 00:06:11,600
The verdict column contains the allowances and the refusals side by
side, including the refusals from a few moments ago in this

60
00:06:11,600 --> 00:06:12,255
recording.

61
00:06:12,775 --> 00:06:19,500
The entries are hash chained, so a record cannot be quietly edited
after the fact without breaking the chain.

62
00:06:20,020 --> 00:06:26,395
Institutions run on paper as much as on tables, so the same
governance has to reach the documents.

63
00:06:26,915 --> 00:06:35,440
285 scanned pages across 18 batches, each one read, classified,
summarized and scored for confidence.

64
00:06:35,960 --> 00:06:44,111
Look at the disposition column. The platform did not classify
everything and declare success. It sorted its own output into what

65
00:06:44,111 --> 00:06:50,160
it is confident about and what a person has to check, and the
confidence score is what decides.

66
00:06:50,680 --> 00:06:57,355
80 pages are sitting in a review queue right now, each with the
reason it landed there recorded next to it.

67
00:06:57,875 --> 00:07:06,401
A person accepts or reprocesses, and that decision is written to
the audit trail. Nothing crosses from machine output to trusted

68
00:07:06,401 --> 00:07:07,600
record without it.

69
00:07:08,120 --> 00:07:13,270
Data does not arrive on its own, so the last piece is the pipeline
that moves it.

70
00:07:13,790 --> 00:07:22,390
This is the pipeline builder on the same tenant, signed in, because
authoring is a write and the public view is deliberately read only.

71
00:07:22,910 --> 00:07:29,810
The panel on the left is the step library. Control flow, code and
models, connectors, and data steps.

72
00:07:30,330 --> 00:07:39,310
Four steps for a nightly student information system load. Pull the
extract. Normalize the rows. A quality gate. Publish the result to

73
00:07:39,310 --> 00:07:40,255
the lakehouse.

74
00:07:40,775 --> 00:07:49,009
Each step is dropped onto the canvas and then wired to the next
one, so the order of operations is an explicit graph rather than

75
00:07:49,009 --> 00:07:50,875
something buried in a script.

76
00:07:51,395 --> 00:08:00,258
The quality gate is a real condition rather than a label. It reads
the rejected row count from the step before it and continues only

77
00:08:00,258 --> 00:08:01,870
when that count is zero.

78
00:08:02,390 --> 00:08:10,290
Name the flow, and save it. It becomes draft version one on this
tenant, and the audit trail records who created it and when.

79
00:08:10,810 --> 00:08:16,410
A pipeline here is an object in the platform with a version, an
owner, and a history.

80
00:08:16,930 --> 00:08:24,964
Here is what one looks like after it has been running for a while.
This is not a picture of a pipeline, it is the pipeline, read live

81
00:08:24,964 --> 00:08:25,930
from the engine.

82
00:08:26,450 --> 00:08:34,141
Collect the counters for the day, summarize them, then a real
branch. If the backlog is healthy, publish to the morning

83
00:08:34,141 --> 00:08:37,825
dashboard. If it is not, escalate to the duty supervisor.

84
00:08:38,345 --> 00:08:45,745
The run history sits behind the graph, so the question of whether
last night worked is one click, not a support ticket.

85
00:08:46,265 --> 00:08:54,651
Everything so far has been the inside of the platform. This is what
it looks like when the output is published to a community that did

86
00:08:54,651 --> 00:08:56,090
not ask for a database.

87
00:08:56,610 --> 00:09:02,485
This is a school district transparency dashboard, open to the
public, with no sign in.

88
00:09:03,005 --> 00:09:11,334
Daily attendance is plotted against a labeled axis, and the
district is compared year over year, so a parent can see the trend

89
00:09:11,334 --> 00:09:13,780
rather than a single headline number.

90
00:09:14,300 --> 00:09:24,291
Underneath, every school is ranked with its rate printed next to
it. The top school this year sits at 94.7 percent and the bottom at

91
00:09:24,291 --> 00:09:25,275
89.7 percent.

92
00:09:25,795 --> 00:09:32,870
The order is not the same in both years, which is the kind of
movement a published figure is supposed to make visible.

93
00:09:33,390 --> 00:09:37,590
Change one filter and the whole page narrows to a single school.

94
00:09:38,110 --> 00:09:47,344
Enrollment falls to 195 and the chart rescales its axis instead of
flattening. Proficiency and readiness come back as four separate

95
00:09:47,344 --> 00:09:51,010
measures, each with its value printed above the bar.

96
00:09:51,530 --> 00:10:00,630
Behavior is reported per 100 students, and this school runs at 20.3
incidents against a district figure of 38.8.

97
00:10:01,150 --> 00:10:07,075
The grade filter also repopulates, offering only the grades this
school actually serves.

98
00:10:07,595 --> 00:10:12,995
Now look at the climate and engagement survey for that same school.
Every row is masked.

99
00:10:13,515 --> 00:10:21,353
The mark is a shield reading less than or equal to ten, and the
platform is careful about what it means. Withheld to protect

100
00:10:21,353 --> 00:10:26,915
student privacy, because the group is smaller than the minimum
reporting size. Not zero.

101
00:10:27,435 --> 00:10:33,510
The distinction between withheld and zero is preserved so the
published figure is never false.

102
00:10:34,030 --> 00:10:44,614
District wide, the tiered support summary is fully reportable. 78.3
percent of students in tier one, 16.9 percent in tier two, 4.9

103
00:10:44,614 --> 00:10:46,405
percent in tier three.

104
00:10:46,925 --> 00:10:54,625
Break it down by school and most of it disappears, because most
schools have too few students in a tier to report safely.

105
00:10:55,145 --> 00:11:03,975
The platform does not leave that as a gap. It names exactly what it
withheld: which 15 of the 18 schools, which grades, and how many of

106
00:11:03,975 --> 00:11:05,545
the effectiveness rates.

107
00:11:06,065 --> 00:11:14,361
An evaluator can therefore tell the difference between data that is
missing and data that is protected, which is the whole argument for

108
00:11:14,361 --> 00:11:15,590
publishing this way.

109
00:11:16,110 --> 00:11:25,735
